    "title": "EPA to allow E15 gasoline sales nationwide this summer",
    "text": "The EPA will allow summer sales of E15 gasoline nationwide, aiming to increase fuel supply and give drivers lower-cost options during peak travel season. The Environmental Protection Agency has announced it will allow nationwide summer sales of higher-ethanol gasoline blends in a move aimed at boosting fuel supply and easing costs ahead of peak driving season. EPA Administrator Lee Zeldin issued a temporary emergency fuel waiver allowing the sale of E15, which is gasoline blended with 15% ethanol, and removing certain federal restrictions on E10 fuel, gasoline blended with 10% ethanol. The action is set to take effect May 1. Officials said the waiver is intended to help prevent fuel shortages and give drivers more options at the pump as demand rises during the summer travel season. The EPA said E15 is already sold at more than 3,000 stations nationwide and is typically priced lower than standard E10 gasoline. “EPA is working with our federal partners to reduce unnecessary costs and uncertainty and ensure that gas prices remain affordable for all Americans through the summer,” Zeldin said in a statement. He added that the emergency action would increase fuel supply and expand consumer choice. Under federal rules, E15 sales are often limited in warmer months because of concerns about fuel volatility and smog formation. The new waiver temporarily relaxes those restrictions and allows gasoline with 9% to 15% ethanol content to be distributed nationwide under a single fuel standard. The measure is initially scheduled to remain in place through May 20, which is the maximum period allowed under the Clean Air Act, although EPA officials said the agency could extend the waiver if fuel supply conditions warrant it. The announcement comes as gasoline prices remain elevated in many parts of the country. The national average for regular gas hovers just below $4 per gallon, according to AAA, with diesel prices climbing even faster amid global oil market uncertainty and rising seasonal demand. Administration officials and agricultural leaders said expanding E15 availability could also increase demand for corn-based ethanol and support domestic energy production. Critics in past debates have argued that wider ethanol use can raise environmental concerns tied to air quality and water use. According to the Renewable Fuels Association, E15 is fully approved for use in vehicles manufactured after the year 2000. That equates to more than 96% of vehicles on the road right now.",
